chuckcar wrote:
> So a monster right behind it (like a pony, mumak, giant et al) won't be
> a problem if your ac goes up by 6-9 points? I doubt that...

Only in incredibly contrived circumstances could a rust monster take
off 6 points of AC, let alone 9, even if you stupidly stand toe-to-toe
in melee with it.

Your shirt is unrustable.  Same for your cloak.  The armor you wear is
either studded leather, mithril, or DSM except in the very early
(pre-rust monster) game.

That leaves a shield, helm, gloves, and boots.  None of the ones you
want long-term 2 points of AC to lose to rust:
shield: Small shield or shield of reflection (latter unrustable) are
the choices
Gloves: Except in the early game, dex or leather gloves (both
unrustable).  Even with GoP, only 1 point.
Helm: Brilliance or telepathy (1 point of AC rustable) or cornuthuam or
leather helm (unrustable)
Boots: Speed or jumping (1 point rustable) or elven boots.

I suppose if you had a small shield, gauntlets of power, a helm of say
telepathy, and speed boots you could lose 4 points of AC.  That depends
on the rust monsters successfully hitting all of them independently.

In real life, you'll Elbereth and take down the rust monster--and even
if you're stuck with finger-elbereth, it's unlikely to rust more than
1-2 items of armor.

Wasting a ?enchant armor on an interim item (like, say, a large shield,
dwarven shoes, or a dwarven helm) is exactly that: a waste, and not
conducive to ascension.

That's all almost beside the point, though.  Suppose you have a
?enchant armor.  You have 2 options:
1. Read it for AC.  Your +0 speed boots (1 AC bonus) become something
between +1 and +3 speed boots (2-4 AC bonus).  If you run into that
rust monster, they drop by 1 (to 1-3 AC bonus)
2. Read it confused, for rustproofing.  Your boots remain +1, now and
later.  So they're worse now than if you used it for AC, and probably
worse later (and certainly not better later).

There's no case in which enchanting your base ac:1
helm/boots/glove/shield isn't better now and at least as good later as
rustproofing it.  Using ?enchant armor to rustproof before you've
enchanted everything to the max is a waste.

The sole exceptions are:
1. You have an AC2 or better item (in real life, a robe is the only one
that makes sense).  In this case, once it's +2 or better, perhaps
fireproofing it makes sense.
2. You have waterwalking boots.  Fireproofing them lets you walk on
lava.

Otherwise, the only time fooproofing your armor is worth it at all is
with a cursed ?destroy armor.  In practice I find it's not worth the
trouble (or the risk of taking off the cloak of magic resistance,
shield of reflection, etc) but I can easily see others thinking it's
worthwile.

Fooproofing a good weapon _is_ worth it, but the only weapons you'll
keep for all that long are likely to come fooproofed (sacrifice gifts,
dipping for Excalibur).  If you find a randomly generated artifact (or
bones file or whatever) then fooproofing it once it's +2 or better is
worthwile.  Especially since ?enchant weapon are relatively abundant
(given the number of them you need) compared to ?enchant armor.



